// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  FRACTAL EXPLORER — a Garden panel app written in Petal.
//
//  An escape-time renderer for the Mandelbrot set, its Julia sets, the
//  Burning Ship and the Tricorn, with smooth colouring, wheel zoom about the
//  cursor, drag and keyboard pan, and a tile cache that survives a pan.
//
//  Exercises: custom rendering, zoom/pan, computation.
//
//  A script cannot compute a full-resolution image inside one frame, and a
//  panel has no canvas that survives the frame, so the renderer is built
//  around two caches:
//
//    - SAMPLES. The plane is cut into 64 px tiles on a lattice anchored at
//      the origin. A tile holds 32 x 32 smooth iteration counts and is
//      refined in four interlaced passes (16, 8, 4, 2 px cells), each reusing
//      the samples of the one before. Every frame spends a budget of
//      iterations on the coarsest unfinished tile nearest the pointer. A pan
//      moves the lattice under the view, so only the tiles that scroll in
//      are new work.
//
//    - DRAW LISTS. One draw call per sample would be 170,000 calls a frame.
//      Each finished tile is instead fitted, row by row, with piecewise
//      linear segments of the palette phase: a run of the set's interior is
//      one rect, and a smooth stretch of the exterior is one gradient rect.
//      The segments store the phase, not a colour, so a palette change, the
//      band density and colour cycling cost no recomputation at all. Where
//      the picture is all filaments nothing merges, so a draw-call budget
//      holds the busiest tiles at 4 px. Each tile is drawn by one function
//      call, which Petal's memoized scopes replay untouched on any frame
//      where that tile's list and position did not change.
//
//  A zoom keeps the old image on screen, scaled about the cursor, until the
//  new tiles are at least as sharp as it is.
//
//  Designed for a 1320x900 viewport.  See README.md.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ── Chrome palette ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

